{
  "id": "sqrt",
  "questionId": "sqrt",
  "slug": "sqrt",
  "title": "Sqrt(x)",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search",
    "math"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "instead of checking every candidate answer starting from 0, notice that as a candidate integer grows, its square grows too - that monotonic relationship is what makes binary search possible over the space of possible answers",
    "binary search on a range from 0 to x: for a candidate mid, compare mid * mid to x to decide whether the true answer is smaller, larger, or exactly mid",
    "keep track of the largest mid seen so far whose square is <= x, and narrow the search range each iteration",
    "for large x near the top of the constraint range, squaring mid in a 32-bit integer type can overflow - use a wider integer type for the multiplication, or compare mid to x / mid instead of mid * mid to x",
    "the search range can start tighter than [0, x] - for x >= 2, the answer is always less than x / 2 + 1, which shrinks the initial range"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using a built-in sqrt or pow function, which the problem explicitly disallows",
    "overflow when computing mid * mid for large x, since mid and x can both be near 2^31 - 1",
    "off-by-one, returning the smallest i where i * i > x instead of the largest i where i * i <= x",
    "not handling x = 0 or x = 1 as valid inputs where the answer equals x itself"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a non-negative integer `x`.\n\n**Return the square root of x rounded down to the nearest integer.**\n\nYou are not allowed to use any built-in exponent or square root function such as pow or sqrt; compute it yourself.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= x <= 2^31 - 1",
      "no built-in square root or power function may be used",
      "the result is the floor of the true, real-valued square root"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "x = 0 (answer is 0)",
      "x = 1 (answer is 1)",
      "x is a perfect square (the true root is returned exactly)",
      "x is just below or just above a perfect square, testing rounding-down at the boundary",
      "x is near the top of the input range, where naively squaring a candidate could overflow a 32-bit integer"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
